`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Every way to choose N and D fits one playbook",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"From here on, N counts a model's parameters, D its training tokens and C its training compute in FLOPs. Week 2 wrote the tokens as T, and Week 6 used N for the number of GPUs, so in this lecture N always means parameters."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Choosing N and D for a run you can afford only once depends on a chain of three links. The compute budget constrains N and D, because every token costs compute and a larger model costs more per token. Under a training recipe kept comparable across runs, N and D predict the final loss. A benchmark protocol then maps the model's behavior on a task to a score."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The last link is the fragile one. Compute maps to loss through a smooth regularity that a fit can follow. The map from a task's loss to the accuracy reported on that task is loose. It also depends on how the score is computed."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 5's warmup-stable-decay (WSD) schedule makes the sweep cheaper: the runs of one model size share one long stable phase and branch off only for their short decays. A score means something only together with the protocol that produced it: the prompt, the solved examples placed in it, and the rule that turns the model's output into a score."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each approach you might try belongs to one step of this playbook. Extending the trend of small models to the target budget is the fit and the extrapolation. Training several sizes at one budget and keeping the best is a sweep. Fitting loss against model size at each budget then reads off the best size. A rule of thumb for tokens per parameter summarizes such fits, and the sweep's data can test whether it holds. Keeping the small runs comparable is a condition on the sweep. Trying the forecast on a medium-sized run first is the held-out check. Asking whether a low loss means a good model is the measurement step."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Two new modules: fit and measure",accent:5,number:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
