`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A power law is a straight line on log-log axes",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. trained Transformer language models over a wide range of sizes and plotted the test loss against each of the three quantities. Across the ranges they measured, each relation is close to a straight line on log-log axes ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),". Equation (1) is an empirical fit, not a law derived from first principles. A fitted power law of this kind, which predicts loss from scale, is what we call a scaling law."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each form holds when the resource it names is the bottleneck and the others are abundant. The first form, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L(N)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes models trained on enough data that data does not limit them. The second, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L(D)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes models large enough that size does not limit them, and the third, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L(C)",display:!1,trail:","})," describes runs that spend their compute efficiently."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Why a straight line? Take the logarithm of the first form. The logarithm turns the power into a product and the quotient into a difference:"}),e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log L = \\alpha_N \\log N_c - \\alpha_N \\log N."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Plotted against ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log N",display:!1,trail:","})," this is a line with slope ",e.jsx(s,{math:"-\\alpha_N",display:!1,trail:"."})," The constant ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N_c",display:!1})," appears only in the term ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\alpha_N \\log N_c",display:!1,trail:","})," which shifts the line up or down. The same step works for D and C. A straight line is easy to fit and easy to extend, which is why scaling fits are made on log-log axes."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The step needs (1) as written. A full description of the loss adds a constant term, the floor ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E",display:!1,trail:":"})," the irreducible loss that more scale cannot remove. Equation (1) leaves it out, as Kaplan et al.'s fits over their range do. With ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E",display:!1})," added, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log L",display:!1})," is no longer linear in ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log N",display:!1,trail:","})," and the curve flattens as the power-law term shrinks toward zero."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The loss ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L",display:!1})," is the cross-entropy measured on held-out text, in nats per token. The fitted exponents are all below 0.1, and compute has the smallest, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\alpha_C \\approx 0.050",display:!1,trail:"."})," A small exponent means that loss falls slowly with scale. Under ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L(C) \\propto C^{-0.05}",display:!1,trail:","})," ten times more compute multiplies the modeled loss term by only ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{-0.05} \\approx 0.89",display:!1,trail:","})," an 11% reduction."]}),e.jsxs(o,{title:"Kaplan et al.'s fitted constants",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each constant has the unit of the variable it scales."}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
