`]})]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. count N as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N_\\text{nonemb}",display:!1,trail:","})," without the embeddings. The plot's legend counts ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N_\\text{total}",display:!1,trail:","})," every parameter. ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N_\\text{total}",display:!1})," includes the tied output head, also called the unembedding, which shares its weight matrix with the input embedding."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The plot shows a sweep of the kind such a fit starts from: loss against training tokens D on log-log axes, one curve per model size. Every curve falls as its model sees more tokens and then bends. With the model size fixed, the curve flattens at a floor of its own: the irreducible loss plus the extra loss the model pays for its limited size. Larger models therefore lie lower and flatten at a lower level."}),e.jsx(o,{title:"How the plot is drawn",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"All curves come from one parametric loss model, so the plot shows the shape of a sweep, without the noise of real runs."})})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{reading:!0,title:"Three regimes, one curve",accent:0,number:"8",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
