`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A loss curve has three regions, and the power law is the middle one",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A single loss curve has three regimes, each a stretch of the curve ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hestness2017",children:"Hestness et al., 2017"}),". In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"small-data region"}),", the model has seen too little data for the fitted scaling relation to hold, and its loss is data-limited and noisy. In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"power-law region"}),", the curve is approximately a straight line on log-log axes, and (1) describes it. In the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"irreducible-error region"}),", the curve approaches an empirical floor under the fixed data distribution and objective. Change the training data or what the model is trained to predict, and the floor can move."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Why should the middle be a power law at all? An illustrative analogy is mean estimation. Suppose you estimate a mean from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n",display:!1})," samples drawn from a distribution with variance ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sigma^2",display:!1,trail:"."})," The squared error of that estimate falls like ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sigma^2/n",display:!1,trail:"."})," Use that estimate to predict a fresh sample, and the expected squared error is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sigma^2 + \\sigma^2/n",display:!1,trail:"."})," The second term falls as a power of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n",display:!1,trail:","})," and the first is a floor that no amount of data removes. The analogy shows only this shape. It derives no neural scaling law, and its mechanism does not carry over. Its exponent is 1, while the language-model exponents are below 0.1. The fitted floor is a constant estimated by a fit, and without further assumptions it is not the entropy of the data."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Compare fitted lines, not single runs",figure:e.jsx(T,{}),figureWidth:"1/2",accent:1,number:"9",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
