`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A fitted line decides between designs at the target scale",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A scaling law turns one-off comparisons into engineering decisions. You compare two architectures or two optimizers by the slope and offset of their fitted lines, because the line extends to the scale you cannot afford to run. The offset summarizes the difference at a reference scale, and the exponent summarizes how fast loss falls. A decision for the target scale needs both."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The figure is an illustrative case. Both axes are logarithmic, so each design's fitted power law is a straight line. Design A starts lower, and design B falls more steeply. Look at where the two lines cross: to the right of the shaded band of runs you can afford, and to the left of the dashed target. Every run in the band ranks A first, yet the fits predict that B wins at the target. A comparison of single runs would have picked the wrong design."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. give an example. In the setting they tested, Transformers and LSTMs differ in how their loss scales on tokens late in the context. Extrapolated curves can identify a likely crossover before a run at the target scale ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),". Such a forecast reaches beyond the fitted range, so a held-out run there has to confirm the crossover before you rely on it."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"What counts as a parameter?",accent:2,number:"10",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
