`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Small scale: counting embeddings or not moves the fitted exponent ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsx(c,{type:"warning",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Not settled: the exercise fits both conventions"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Kaplan counted for clean curves, the course counts what the GPUs spend",children:[e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Two conventions and their reasons"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan et al. leave the vocabulary and position embeddings out of N, and they compute C as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6N",display:!1})," per token from that N. Their reason was empirical. Plotted against all parameters, models of equal size but different depth spread out. Plotted against non-embedding parameters, they fall on one curve, except models with fewer than two layers or extreme depth-to-width ratios ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course counts every parameter once, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N_\\text{total}",display:!1,trail:","})," with the tied output head included. It takes C from the FLOPs per token each model executes, because a sweep's budget is the compute the GPUs spend. The output head's matmul, which scores every vocabulary entry at each position, is real compute and a large share of a small model's total. In Kaplan's fit, the best model size grows fast with the budget. Porian et al. find three causes for that, and one is leaving the head's FLOPs out of C ",e.jsx(a,{id:"porian2024",children:"Porian et al., 2024"}),". The input lookup costs no matmul FLOPs, because it only selects one row of the embedding matrix. Kaplan's reason matters less in the course's sweep. Its width-from-depth rule, 64 channels per layer, gives every size the same shape."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Why the convention matters at small scale"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The embedding grows only with the width, while the Transformer blocks grow with width and depth, so the embedding's share shrinks as models grow. In the course's sweep, with a vocabulary of 8,192 tokens, the tied embedding is about 76% of the parameters at depth 2 and about 11% at depth 10 (our arithmetic from the sweep's configurations). Switching conventions moves each model's point on a log axis by the logarithm of the ratio between its total and its non-embedding count. That ratio is large for small models and close to 1 for large ones, so a line that is straight in one convention bends in the other. Schaeffer et al. state the general case. Including or leaving out the embedding acts roughly like adding a constant to every parameter count, and an added constant changes the fitted exponent. A constant factor changes only the fitted prefactor ",e.jsx(a,{id:"schaeffer2025chinchilla",children:"Schaeffer et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exponent at stake is the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"allocation exponent"})," a in ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N^* \\propto C^{a}",display:!1,trail:","})," which says how fast the compute-optimal model size ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N^*",display:!1})," grows with the budget. Kaplan et al. found ",e.jsx(s,{math:"a = 0.73",display:!1,trail:"."})," In Pearce and Song's own runs of 0.8M to 4.6M parameters, counting total parameters gave ",e.jsx(s,{math:"a = 0.49",display:!1,trail:","})," and counting without embeddings gave ",e.jsx(s,{math:"a = 0.74",display:!1,trail:"."})," They recommend that scaling studies count total parameters and compute ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Pearce and Song's simulation",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Pearce and Song also simulate Chinchilla's loss model at Kaplan's model sizes, counting without embeddings. They get an allocation exponent of 0.74 to 0.78 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"}),"."]})}),e.jsx(o,{title:"A third convention",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek LLM uses non-embedding FLOPs per token. It counts the attention's work and leaves out the output head's matmul over the vocabulary. Its authors compare it with two parameter-based estimates, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6N",display:!1})," with the embedding and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6N",display:!1})," without it. In small models, each differs from the FLOPs count by up to 50% ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2024deepseekllm",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024"}),"."]})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exercise fits the same sweep under both conventions: every parameter with the counted FLOPs per token, attention included, and Kaplan's non-embedding N with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C = 6ND",display:!1,trail:"."})," Week 2's coarse estimate ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C \\approx 6ND",display:!1})," uses whichever N you put into it. Whenever you write it, name the convention for N."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Even with consistent accounting, a smooth loss does not guarantee a smooth downstream metric. A downstream metric is a task score, such as benchmark accuracy, as opposed to the loss ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tay2022",children:"Tay et al., 2022"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
