`]}),e.jsx(c,{type:"key",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"About 20 tokens per parameter, in Chinchilla's setup"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Kaplan puts most new compute into parameters, Chinchilla splits it evenly",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Under Kaplan et al.'s exponents, ten times more compute buys a model about ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{0.73} \\approx 5.4",display:!1})," times larger, trained on only about ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{0.27} \\approx 1.9",display:!1})," times more tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"How each study found the optimum"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Almost every Kaplan run used one schedule: 3,000 warmup steps, then a cosine decay to zero over ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2.5 \\times 10^5",display:!1})," steps. Kaplan et al. took the lower envelope of all training curves, as in Approach 1, but read it off these long schedules. A checkpoint partway through a long run thus stood in for a shorter run ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kaplan2020",children:"Kaplan et al., 2020"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Hoffmann et al. matched each run's cosine schedule to its own length and estimated the split with the three approaches from the previous slide ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". Under all three, N and D grow at about the same rate, close to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sqrt{C}",display:!1,trail:"."})," The compute-optimal point is about ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"20 tokens per parameter"}),". The 70B Chinchilla model, trained on 1.4T tokens, has exactly that ratio: ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1.4\\text{T} / 70\\text{B} = 20",display:!1,trail:"."})]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Chinchilla's runs and setup",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Hoffmann et al. trained over 400 models, from 70M to over 16B parameters, on 5B to 500B tokens of MassiveText. Each cosine schedule lowered the learning rate tenfold. The models were dense decoder-only Transformers like Gopher's, and the final Chinchilla model used a SentencePiece tokenizer with 32,000 tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),"."]})}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Why the exponents differ"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Hoffmann et al. blamed the fixed schedule and Kaplan's small models. A checkpoint partway through a schedule planned for a longer run has a higher loss than a run whose schedule ends there, so short runs look worse than they are and the fit favors large models on few tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". Porian et al. reproduce Kaplan's exponent and then remove the gap with three changes ",e.jsx(a,{id:"porian2024",children:"Porian et al., 2024"}),". They count the output head's FLOPs. They scale the warmup with the model size. They tune the learning rate, the batch size and AdamW's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\beta_2",display:!1})," at each size. Matching the learning-rate decay to each run turns out not to be essential, against Hoffmann et al.'s explanation. Pearce and Song attribute much of the gap to counting without embeddings at small scale ",e.jsx(a,{id:"pearce2024",children:"Pearce & Song, 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"Is the exponent of about 0.5 a coincidence?"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["That N and D grow at about the same rate is an empirical regularity. Its exponent carries over to a new setup better than its constant does. The exponent near 0.5 recurs in careful fits on web text: in Chinchilla's first two approaches, in Besiroglu et al.'s refit of the third ",e.jsx(a,{id:"besiroglu2024",children:"Besiroglu et al., 2024"}),", and in other labs' IsoFLOP fits. In Llama 3's, the tokens grow as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C^{0.53}",display:!1,trail:","})," so N grows about as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C^{0.47}",display:!1})," (our arithmetic) ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". In DeepSeek LLM's, model scale measured in FLOPs per token grows as ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C^{0.52}",display:!1})," ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2024deepseekllm",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024"}),". Kaplan's 0.73 came from the method. The exponent itself can move with the data: DeepSeek LLM's exponent for model scale rose from 0.45 to 0.58 as data quality rose ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2024deepseekllm",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024"}),". In the surface picture, an exponent of 0.5 needs ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\alpha = \\beta",display:!1,trail:":"})," the size penalty and the data penalty shrink at the same rate. No theory says they must."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The constant does not carry over. A scaling law belongs to its data, tokenizer and model family, so outside Chinchilla's setup 20 tokens per parameter is an approximation. Llama 3's own fit forecast 402B parameters on 16.55T tokens at Llama 3's budget, about 41 tokens per parameter (our arithmetic) ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". Open Athena's IsoFLOP curves put its mixture-of-experts (MoE) models near 60 tokens per active parameter, the parameters one token passes through ",e.jsx(a,{id:"dial2026marin535b",children:"Dial, 2026"}),". Tokens are units of a tokenizer, so the ratio also moves with the tokenizer."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"While the exponent stays near 0.5, the ratio stays about constant across budgets, so a few small budgets can set it. In practice, assume an exponent near 0.5 for your own data, architecture and tokenizer, then check it and the ratio with a small IsoFLOP sweep."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{reading:!0,title:"Fitted exponents are fragile",accent:6,number:"15",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Warmup, output-layer FLOPs, counting: each shifts the exponent"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Optimizer tuning that changes with size biases the fit"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Refit under each convention, report how far exponents move"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Audit how a fit was made before comparing its exponent",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kaplan's 0.73 and Chinchilla's roughly 0.5 say little about each other until you know what each fit assumed. Each of Porian et al.'s contributors to the gap distorts a fit in its own way ",e.jsx(a,{id:"porian2024",children:"Porian et al., 2024"}),". Kaplan's fixed warmup of 3,000 steps takes up much of the short runs that are best at small budgets. To get past the warmup, those runs need more tokens, which biases the fit toward more tokens at small budgets. Beyond the warmup the optimal token count grows only slowly, so the fitted model size grows fast. Omitting the FLOPs of the output head, the unembedding matmul that Porian et al. call the last layer, shifts the compute axis. If AdamW's hyperparameters are tuned differently at each model size, the tuning itself can change which size wins at a given budget. Together with the parameter-counting convention, these factors help reconcile the two estimates."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Before you compare two fitted exponents, check how each study scales its warmup, whether it counts the head FLOPs, which parameter convention it uses and how its optimizer hyperparameters change with model size. Where several conventions are defensible, refit under each one and report how far the exponent moves. One fit rarely justifies two decimal places."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Train-optimal is not deploy-optimal",figure:e.jsx(L,{}),figureLayout:"wrap",figureWidth:"3/5",accent:0,number:"16",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Chinchilla: best loss for this training budget"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Each served token costs about ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2N",display:!1})," FLOPs"]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Heavy serving: overtrain a smaller model, far past 20 tokens per parameter"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Llama 3 8B: about 1,875 tokens per parameter ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Production models train far past 20 tokens per parameter",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Chinchilla's rule counts training compute alone. A model that will be deployed has a second cost, because every token it serves needs a forward pass. The count behind formula (2) prices a forward pass at about 2 FLOPs per parameter per token, so a smaller model is cheaper on every token it serves. Here, ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"overtraining"})," means training beyond the Chinchilla training-compute optimum to improve the deployment objective. When the expected inference volume is high, extra pretraining can be traded for a smaller served model."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The figure places models by release date on the horizontal axis and by training tokens per parameter on the vertical axis, which is logarithmic. The dashed line marks Chinchilla's 20 tokens per parameter. Two points anchor the training-compute view. GPT-3 trained on about 1.7 tokens per parameter, well below the line ",e.jsx(a,{id:"gpt3-2020",children:"Brown et al., 2020"}),". Chinchilla lies on it at 20 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". LLaMA-65B, at about 22 tokens per parameter, is an early example of overtraining ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama1-2023",children:"Touvron et al., 2023a"}),". Llama 2 70B, at about 29, is another ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama2-2023",children:"Touvron et al., 2023b"}),". Llama 3 went much further. Its 70B model trained on about 215 tokens per parameter and its 8B model on about 1,875, both from the report's rounded token counts ",e.jsx(a,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Marin: a smaller model, overtrained on purpose",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Marin 535B-A23B, a mixture-of-experts model with 23B active parameters, trained on 18T tokens, about 13 times its compute-optimal token count of about 1.4T. The lab reports that overtraining by 10 times costs roughly a factor of 2 in compute efficiency: the loss it reaches would have cost about half the compute at the optimum. It took that price for a smaller model, which is cheaper on every served token, and its hardware set how large the model could be ",e.jsx(a,{id:"dial2026marin535b",children:"Dial, 2026"}),"."]})}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The 8B model's ratio is far beyond the 20-token training-compute reference, but the ratio alone does not show that the extra training was worth it. The deployment optimum depends on several factors: traffic, hardware, latency targets, the per-token serving cost and the added training cost. A ratio of tokens to parameters records none of them."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(h,{tab:"The Playbook",plate:"ercker-silver-assay",children:[e.jsxs(n,{title:"Keep the recipe comparable across the sweep",figure:e.jsx(b,{}),figureWidth:"1/2",accent:2,number:"17",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Fixed: data, model shape, schedule, measurement"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Learning rate checked per size: one fits all five sizes"}),`
