`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Rubrics let a model grade open-ended work criterion by criterion",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A grader turns a model's output into a score. Code graders (unit tests, exact matching, environment-state checks) are fast, cheap and reproducible but reject correct answers in unexpected forms and cannot judge open-ended text ",e.jsx(a,{id:"anthropic2026demystifying",children:"Anthropic, 2026a"}),". Model graders handle open-ended output at scale but bring biases and need calibration. Human experts are the reference and the most expensive option."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Many 2026 benchmarks grade against a ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"rubric"}),": a list of specific pass-or-fail criteria written for each task. HealthBench holds 5,000 medical conversations with rubrics written by 262 physicians, 48,562 criteria in total ",e.jsx(a,{id:"arora2025healthbench",children:"Arora et al., 2025"}),". GPT-4.1 checks each criterion separately. Its agreement with physicians matched the physicians' agreement with each other. In GDPval, human experts compare work products such as slide decks, spreadsheets or diagrams without knowing which came from a model ",e.jsx(a,{id:"gdpval2025",children:"Patwardhan et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Judges need checks. Calibrate against human experts, grade each rubric dimension in a separate call, and let the judge answer "Unknown" when evidence is missing ',e.jsx(a,{id:"anthropic2026demystifying",children:"Anthropic, 2026a"}),". Zheng et al. found three biases in LLM judges: they favor the answer shown first, longer answers, and their own answers ",e.jsx(a,{id:"zheng2023judging",children:"Zheng et al., 2023"}),". A panel of judges from several model families reduces self-preference ",e.jsx(a,{id:"aa-methodology",children:"Artificial Analysis, n.d.-b"}),". None of these is a complete fix, so validate any judging protocol against the decision it supports."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Rubric and bias examples",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each HealthBench criterion carries a weight between −10 and 10, and criteria that describe behavior to avoid get negative weights ",e.jsx(a,{id:"arora2025healthbench",children:"Arora et al., 2025"}),". GDPval's automated grader agreed with the experts 66% of the time, 5 points below expert–expert agreement ",e.jsx(a,{id:"gdpval2025",children:"Patwardhan et al., 2025"}),". Harvey's legal benchmark, run by Artificial Analysis, grades each deliverable against 44 to 90 criteria with a panel of three judge models. Its score is the share of judges that find every criterion passed, averaged over tasks, and a task with a material hallucination counts as zero ",e.jsx(a,{id:"aa-methodology",children:"Artificial Analysis, n.d.-b"}),". Length-controlled AlpacaEval removes the length bias with a regression ",e.jsx(a,{id:"dubois2024alpacaeval",children:"Dubois et al., 2024"}),". Arena turns human votes between two anonymous answers into ratings with a Bradley-Terry model and adds style as a variable to that regression ",e.jsx(a,{id:"chiang2024arena",children:"Chiang et al., 2024"})," ",e.jsx(a,{id:"arena-style-control",children:"Arena Team, 2024"}),"."]})})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{reading:!0,title:"MMLU-Pro: one name, two protocols",accent:5,number:"29",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
