`]}),e.jsx(c,{type:"key",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Report test items, runs and an interval"})}),e.jsx(c,{type:"warning",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A score that becomes a target stops measuring well"})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Precision costs repeated runs, and a published score attracts optimization",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Cost grows with the calls per item, the sampled repeats and the judge calls, and the precision you need sets how many repeats you run. A multiple-choice item costs one forward pass. An agentic task can run for hours, and a leaderboard may require several trials per task ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench-leaderboard",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, n.d."}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Repeats cut sampling noise but add no items. The items are a small sample of the problems the benchmark stands for. The uncertainty from that limited sample remains no matter how often you rerun the same items. On an ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n",display:!1}),"-item evaluator one item is worth ",e.jsx(s,{math:"100/n",display:!1})," points. With 30 items, one item is worth about 3.3 points. Before you read meaning into a small difference, report the number of items, the number of runs, the estimator (for example the mean over runs) and an interval for the score. When you compare models, also report the paired item outcomes: which items each model got right."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Comparisons also wear down the benchmark itself. In the formulation commonly attributed to Strathern, a measure stops being a good measure once it becomes a target ",e.jsx(a,{id:"strathern1997",children:"Strathern, 1997"}),". The idea goes by the name of Goodhart's law. Model developers optimize against a public metric by choosing data, prompts or checkpoints. Every such round uses up some of the resolution the benchmark has left."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Terminal-Bench's trial rules",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Terminal-Bench 4.0, an agentic benchmark of tasks in a terminal, gives every task up to 8 hours. A leaderboard submission must run at least five trials of each task ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench40-2026",children:"Marten, 2026"})," ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench-leaderboard",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, n.d."}),". Version 4.1 changed only how trials are graded, nothing the agent sees ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench41-2026",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, 2026b"}),"."]})})]})]})]}),`
