`,e.jsxs(t.li,{"data-build-step":"3",children:["Fitted surface: Chinchilla's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E + A/N^\\alpha + B/D^\\beta",display:!1,trail:","})," minus a WSD-decay gain"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{"data-build-step":"4",children:["Measured: power law held, both losses below the floor ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E \\approx 2.16",display:!1})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Two forecasts checked by runs they had not seen",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Before reading any result of the two held-out runs, the course team wrote down two forecasts of their final validation loss and committed them to the course repository. Writing a forecast down before the measurement exists is called ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"pre-registration"}),". It prevents the measured loss from shaping the forecast, for example through the choice among several fits."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first forecast uses the exercise's method. For each of the four IsoFLOP curves, take the lowest loss ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L^*",display:!1})," at the bottom of that curve's parabola. Fit a power law in ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C",display:!1})," through these four points and extrapolate to each held-out budget. This power law has no floor. In the figure it is the dashed line, and its forecasts are the diamonds. The second forecast uses Chinchilla's Approach 3 surface, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L(N, D) = E + A/N^\\alpha + B/D^\\beta",display:!1,trail:","})," fitted to the sweep's losses before and after decay. A decay branch ends with a learning-rate drop that lowers the loss. To account for this, the fit predicts each decayed loss as the surface minus one more fitted constant, the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"decay gain"})," (0.23 nats). The surface has a floor ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E",display:!1,trail:","})," so it predicts diminishing returns. Its forecast for a held-out run is the surface at that run's ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"D",display:!1})," minus the decay gain, so both surface forecasts, the squares, lie below the floor."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Both runs came out slightly better than the power law predicted, by 0.007 nats at 10 times and by 0.025 nats at 100 times the sweep's largest budget. The surface missed by 0.25 and 0.49 nats. Both measured losses lie below its fitted floor, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"E \\approx 2.16",display:!1})," nats, which refutes the surface."]}),e.jsxs(o,{title:"The forecasts and the measured losses",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Held-out run"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Measured (nats)"}),e.jsxs(t.th,{children:["Power law ",e.jsx(s,{math:"L^*(C)",display:!1})]}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Fitted surface"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"77M, 10× the sweep's top budget"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.898"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.904"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.151"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"280M, 100× the sweep's top budget"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.552"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"1.577"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"2.046"})]})]})]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The 77M loss is the better result of two learning rates the course tried at that size. The other run, at 1.909, also lies within 0.01 nats of the power law's forecast."})]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Two limits apply. Each measured loss comes from one training run with one seed. The held-out runs also differ from the sweep in their recipe: the learning rate came from the course's fitted rule, while the sweep used one constant rate, and the 280M run trained with a four times larger batch. Part of each miss may therefore come from the recipe, not from the scale."}),e.jsx(o,{title:"What each fit used",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The surface was fitted to the training loss of the sweep's stable runs and the final losses of its decay branches. Both fits left out the smallest size. It was clearly an outlier: its loss dropped suddenly partway through training and lies far above the other sizes."})}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Trust a forecast only once a run it did not see has checked it. The exercise repeats this check with your own fit."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"A consumer's checklist for any evals table",accent:7,number:"41",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Ask"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Setup"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Evaluation software (e.g. lm-eval) and version",e.jsx("br",{}),"Task, data and model revisions",e.jsx("br",{}),"Public or held-out split"]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Protocol"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Shots, chain of thought or thinking effort",e.jsx("br",{}),"Prompt and chat template"]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Scoring"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Scoring rule or grader: tests, a judge model with a rubric, humans"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Agents"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Agent harness (the program around the model), tools, machine resources"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Evidence"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Who ran each number, contamination statement",e.jsx("br",{}),"Number of runs, error bars",e.jsx("br",{}),"Same setup for every model compared"]})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Read an evals table for its setup, protocol, scoring, agents and evidence",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Before you read a gap in a release table as a gap between models, ask the checklist's questions. Each row rules out a different source of the gap."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Setup."})," An update of the evaluation software can change a task's prompt or scoring, so scores from different versions or revisions may not measure the same task. A held-out split is harder to contaminate, and its scores do not compare with scores on the public split."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"An example of a software update",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In 2023, lm-eval-harness rewrote its MMLU task to match the benchmark authors' implementation ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hf-mmlu-three-scores",children:"Fourrier et al., 2023"}),"."]})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Protocol and scoring."})," Without the shots, the prompt, the template, the thinking effort and the scoring rule, you cannot attribute a score difference to the model weights alone. Open-ended tasks add the grader, and scores from different graders do not compare."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Agents."})," An agentic score also depends on the harness, the tools and the machine the agent runs on. Each can move the score while the model stays the same. A score without its harness names no model."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Evidence."})," A competitor's number may be its developer's own report or a rerun in another setup, so ask who ran each one. Without a contamination statement, a high score may come from memorized items. Without the number of runs and error bars, you cannot tell a gap from run-to-run noise."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Use the checklist also when you produce a table: your exercise records its protocol next to every score."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The checklist applies a rule you met with the counting convention for N and C: ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"say which quantity you held fixed, and compare only at that."})," Two losses compare at one compute budget, with parameters counted under one convention. Two scores compare under matched setups. When a field differs between two rows of a table, the gap between their scores belongs to that field as much as to the models. Week 13 meets the rule again when it compares sparse and dense models, which can be matched on total parameters, active parameters or compute."]})]})]})]}),`
