`]}),e.jsx(x,{}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Format, scoring, setup, failure modes and status",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A benchmark score compresses many choices into one number. Five questions recover them."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Format"})," asks what an item looks like and what the model must produce. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Scoring"})," asks how the output becomes a number. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Setup"})," asks what surrounds the model: the examples in the prompt, the template, the tools, the time limit and the number of trials. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Failure modes"})," ask what can make the number wrong without anyone cheating. Examples are fragile answer extraction, contamination, too few items and the agent's scaffold. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Status"})," asks where the benchmark stands today: is it current, saturated at the frontier or retired from a leaderboard? A status always carries a date."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The widget applies these five questions to MMLU-Pro, CritPt and Terminal-Bench 4.1. Each tab shows one item, which answers the format question, and four chips for the other questions. The course wrote every displayed item in its benchmark's format. None is copied from a benchmark. Compare the chips row by row. Scoring differs across the three. For MMLU-Pro, a regular expression extracts the answer letter from the generated chain of thought. CritPt sends each answer to a grading server. Terminal-Bench runs tests in a verifier container."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["CritPt (Complex Research using Integrated Thinking, Physics Test) measures reasoning on unpublished, research-level physics problems across 71 challenges ",e.jsx(a,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),'. A grading server checks every answer: numbers, SymPy expressions and Python functions. The authors publish one example challenge. The solutions to the other 70, the test set, "are kept private to avoid potential contamination" ',e.jsx(a,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),". Answers that were never published cannot appear in training data."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A small set has a price: in an evaluator with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n",display:!1})," items, one item is worth ",e.jsx(s,{math:"100/n",display:!1})," percentage points. For CritPt's 70 test challenges that is about 1.4 points. Artificial Analysis lists CritPt with a private test set in its index v4.3 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"aa-index-v43",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026c"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{optional:!0,title:"Terminal-Bench 4.1 and continuous benchmarking",accent:7,number:"44",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
