`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A Terminal-Bench score belongs to a model, a scaffold and a version",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Terminal-Bench gives an agent a multi-step task in a sandboxed terminal. The agent works in its own container, and a separate verifier container then checks the end state it left behind. No judge model is involved. Keeping the two containers apart blocks many ways of hacking the reward ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"}),". The benchmark measures an ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"agent = model + scaffold"}),", where the scaffold is the program around the model, such as Claude Code or Codex. A score therefore mixes the model's capability with the scaffold's, unless the scaffold is held fixed and reported."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Benchmarks lose value after release. Tasks saturate, maintainers find broken tasks and grading errors, and agents find new ways to hack the reward. Harnesses change as well, which moves scores while the tasks stay the same. Terminal-Bench answers the problems with its tasks through ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"continuous benchmarking"}),': "Benchmarks are software and should be maintained like software" ',e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench-continuous",children:"Marten, n.d."}),". Frequent updates add, remove and fix tasks, and each task carries its own version number. A patch does not change scores, so earlier results stay valid. A minor change touches only the verifier, so saved runs are regraded without new rollouts. A major change alters the agent's environment. Only the tasks whose environment changed are rerun. Each leaderboard belongs to one version of the dataset. A check in continuous integration confirms that an agent told to hack the reward fails."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Version 4.0 (2026-08-28) removed 8 tasks, among them saturated ones, and fixed 19 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench40-2026",children:"Marten, 2026"}),". It left 66 tasks ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench-repo-v400",children:"Marten et al., 2026b"}),". Version 4.1 (2026-11-03) is a minor release: no change the agent sees, only hardened verifiers ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench41-2026",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, 2026b"}),". Where verification runs code the agent wrote, that code now runs as an untrusted user in a separate process, so it cannot read the tests or change the reward file. After the regrade, all 35 agents' scores stayed within the 95% confidence intervals of their 4.0 results. The top entry the post shows, Opus 5.5 in Claude Code at maximum effort, scores 65.76%, so version 4.1 is not saturated."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Why the version number matters: version 2.1 fixed 28 of the 89 tasks in version 2.0, and in the maintainers' paired comparison Claude Code with Opus 4.6 gained 12.1 points with no change to the model ",e.jsx(a,{id:"tbench21-2026",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, 2026a"}),". Without the version number, you cannot tell whether the model or the benchmark moved a score."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{optional:!0,title:"Hinted scaling laws",accent:0,number:"45",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Hints lift small models' scores above zero ",e.jsx(a,{id:"vanadrichem2026hinted",children:"van Adrichem et al., 2026"})]}),`
