`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Fit accuracy in compute and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H",display:!1,trail:","})," then read it at ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H = 0",display:!1})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Delphi model ladder: ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{23}",display:!1}),"-FLOP forecast within 3 percentage points"]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Hints give small models a trend to extrapolate",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A downstream scaling law needs a trend, and small models that score zero on a hard benchmark give none. Van Adrichem et al. turn one benchmark into easier versions indexed by a hint level ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H",display:!1,trail:","})," where ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H = 0",display:!1})," is the original ",e.jsx(a,{id:"vanadrichem2026hinted",children:"van Adrichem et al., 2026"}),". One method shows the first fraction ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H",display:!1})," of a worked solution before the model answers. A second method shows the whole worked solution but masks a fraction ",e.jsx(s,{math:"1 - H",display:!1})," of its words. A third method adds a stronger teacher model's logits to the evaluated model's logits at every decoding step, weighting the teacher's logits by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H",display:!1,trail:"."})," Accuracy rises smoothly with ",e.jsx(s,{math:"H",display:!1,trail:","})," also for models that score near zero without hints, and more hint acts much like more scale."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Delphi, whose recipe Bonus 4 covers, is an open ladder of pretrained models up to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{23}",display:!1})," FLOPs and 25B parameters ",e.jsx(a,{id:"held2026delphi",children:"Held, 2026"}),". The test is GSM8K, a set of grade-school math word problems. A hinted law fitted to Delphi runs of at most ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{20}",display:!1})," FLOPs forecast the ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{23}",display:!1}),"-FLOP model's accuracy of 25.9% to within 3 percentage points. A standard law fitted to the same runs without hints under-forecast it by 13 points. The authors also tested several post-trained model families on the AIME math competition and on Humanity's Last Exam. Across Delphi and these families, the median forecasting error fell from 21 points to 4.3 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"vanadrichem2026hinted",children:"van Adrichem et al., 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The paper is a preprint from November 2026. Of its model families, only Delphi is a ladder trained with one data recipe. The authors therefore cannot yet tell whether the remaining errors come from the method or from quirks of the other families. The post-trained test sets are small: 60 AIME problems and 176 questions from Humanity's Last Exam. Testing the idea on the course's sweep, whose models all score at chance, would make a good final project."})]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{optional:!0,title:"Nanochat finds about 8 tokens per parameter",figure:e.jsx(w,{}),figureWidth:"3/5",accent:7,number:"46",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"IsoFLOP method, as in Chinchilla: fix FLOPs, sweep depth"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Three budgets: optima at 8.1 to 8.5 tokens per parameter"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Course's held-out runs trained on 9 and 11 tokens per parameter"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A recipe-specific constant ",e.jsx(a,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat-miniseries",children:"Karpathy, 2026a"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Nanochat's IsoFLOP fits put the optimum near 8 tokens per parameter",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The same fitting procedure can produce a different allocation constant when the recipe and the scale change. Nanochat, a minimal open codebase for training language models, ran the IsoFLOP method on its own recipe ",e.jsx(a,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat-miniseries",children:"Karpathy, 2026a"}),". It fixed the compute of each run with its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"--target_flops"})," option, swept the model depth, and fitted a quadratic to each IsoFLOP U-curve. Across three budgets, the post prints ratios of 8.1 to 8.5 tokens per parameter at the fitted optima. These ratios count every parameter of the January 2026 recipe. Nanochat's later code changed the architecture and re-fitted the rule. It sets 12 tokens per parameter, but it counts only the parameters of the transformer matrices and the output head. That is a different denominator ",e.jsx(a,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat",children:"Karpathy, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The figure plots the three fitted optima against training compute on a log scale, at ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{18}",display:!1,trail:","})," ",e.jsx(s,{math:"3 \\times 10^{18}",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"6 \\times 10^{18}",display:!1})," FLOPs. The vertical axis is the validation bits per byte, which does not depend on the tokenizer."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The January 2026 maintainer report used one node of eight H100 GPUs for this miniseries. It suggests that Muon, the small model scale or other nanochat specifics could explain the fitted ratio near 8. Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter was fitted with a different recipe, so the two numbers do not conflict. A fitted constant holds for the recipe and the budget range it was measured on."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course's sweep lands close to nanochat. Its IsoFLOP fit with all five sizes, as in the exercise, gives ",e.jsx(s,{math:"N^*(C) \\propto C^{0.505}",display:!1,trail:"."})," The standard error of the exponent is 0.044, so the exponent is consistent with Chinchilla's 0.5. Without the smallest size, the exponent is 0.552. The two held-out runs are the depths nearest to that fit's optimum. The 77M model trained on 868.5M tokens, 11.3 tokens per parameter, and the 280M model on 2.51B tokens, 9.0. Both ratios count every parameter, the embedding included. Our sweep also trains with Muon, and its sizes, 1.4M to 46M parameters, are so small that the tied embedding is a large part of each. Either Muon or the small sizes could lower the ratio. With one optimizer and one size range, the sweep cannot tell which."]})]})]})]}),`
