`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Choose the model from fitted small runs, and know it worked from a reproducible measurement",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Which model."})," You sweep model sizes and token counts at budgets far below the final run, fit power laws in log space, and read the compute-optimal size and token count off the IsoFLOP minima. Chinchilla's minima take only training compute into account, not inference compute ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". A model that will serve many tokens pays for each one again at inference, so it is trained past that optimum, as Llama 3 8B and 70B were. You trust the fit only once it predicts a larger run it was not fitted to. A fair sweep fixes the data, the schedule, the measurement and the width-from-depth rule. It scales the learning rate and the batch size by a rule declared in advance. WSD saves compute because all endpoints of one model size share one stable prefix."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"How you know."})," A score means something only if a gain on the benchmark carries over to the skill it stands for. Leaked test items break that, and so does training aimed at the benchmark alone. Benchmarks must stay hard, informative and relevant, or they retire. The protocol is part of the score: the shots, the chat template, the thinking effort, the tools and the grader. A small model's accuracy can stay at chance while its loss keeps falling. You therefore score the gold answer continuously in bits per byte and choose benchmarks whose signal across models exceeds their noise from checkpoint to checkpoint ",e.jsx(a,{id:"heineman2025signalnoise",children:"Heineman et al., 2025"}),". Both halves rest on one rule: compare only at a quantity held fixed."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The exercise runs both halves in miniature. You fit the course's sweep, predict two held-out runs at 10 and 100 times its largest budget, and measure models with lm-eval-harness pinned at one version, recording the protocol next to every score."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 9 uses the same controlled loop to decide which data reaches training. The model, the recipe and the evaluation stay fixed while the data varies."})]})]}),e.jsx(n,{title:"To-dos until next week",accent:2,number:"48",children:e.jsxs("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-10",children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-24 gap-y-6",children:e.jsxs("figure",{className:"text-center",children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"https://tally.so/r/0V876Q",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"images/qr-week-07-muddiest-point.svg",alt:"QR code for the muddiest-point survey, tally.so/r/0V876Q",className:"w-56 h-56"})}),e.jsxs("figcaption",{children:[e.jsx("strong",{children:"Muddiest point"}),e.jsx("br",{}),"Do it right now! (2 min)"]})]})}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-3",children:[e.jsxs("div",{children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Before the tutorial"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
